Independently authored. Its look and construction grew out of NODE's Measured Light holograms
(same author); no NODE code, fonts, shaders or assets are included. Its working method is
inspired by Anatomy (github.com/wheresryan22/anatomy, MIT): no Anatomy code, assets or text
are included.

Third-party software: none is bundled. The toolkit uses the Python standard library only;
the viewer is plain WebGL2 and uses system fonts. Optional programs it can call when they
are already installed: a Chromium-family browser (review captures) and ffmpeg (video). Each
keeps its own licence and is never downloaded or installed by this toolkit.

Example artwork (construct_hologram/examples/v2/assets) is synthetic, generated by png.py, and
labelled as generated decoration. No authentic artwork, private data, credentials or model
weights are included.

Example constructs (examples/): scenes written by AI models (Claude Opus 5.5, GPT-6.1 Sol, DeepSeek V4.1 Flash)
with Construct, with hero images and animations rendered by Construct's own viewer. They are
independent reconstructions from public references; the scenes cite those references by URL and
include no reference photograph. Product, vehicle and place names belong to their owners, and
no endorsement is implied.

One folder is licensed differently: examples/netlogo-viral-marketing/ derives from NetLogo's
Simple Viral Marketing model, Copyright 2012 Uri Wilensky (NetLogo Models Library; Wilensky &
Rand, An Introduction to Agent-Based Modeling, MIT Press, 2015), licensed under Creative Commons
Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 3.0. Everything in that folder (its page, its JavaScript
port of the model, the exported NetLogo run and its construct) is under CC BY-NC-SA 3.0, not
MIT; see its LICENSE. That page loads three.js from cdn.jsdelivr.net and its fonts from Google
Fonts when opened; nothing from it is bundled into the toolkit or its packages.

History: extracted from the hologram-skill branch of the Tessera project, where the same
